      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "[00:36:16\u201300:36:52] Can we, at least in principle, predict the outcome of tossing a coin? And the answer is yes, we can. We know that coin abuse the loss of mechanics. So even though the initial conditions, how we toss the coin, even though the characteristics of environment, we can, in principle, predict what would be the outcome."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The train arrival time example shows that Bayesian uncertainty decreases with additional information (e.g., screen showing a 5-minute delay)",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "[00:38:54\u201300:39:23] So now again, I still do not know the exact arrival time. I could think that, well, probably it should arrive at 9 minutes past 10. But it may arrive 10 minutes past 10, 11 minutes past 10. So this is still a random variable. But my ignorance has changed. I have observed some additional information, the information from the string."
    },
    {
      "claim": "Frequentist uncertainty is objective (the process is truly random), while Bayesian uncertainty is subjective (due to incomplete information)",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "[00:38:54\u201300:39:23] So this is why it's stated here, subjective. So different observers may have different ignorance on the same random variable."
    },
    {
      "claim": "The coin toss is deterministic in principle (physics determines outcome), but we lack the information to predict it in practice",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "[00:36:16\u201300:36:52] We know that coin abuse the loss of mechanics. So even though the initial conditions, how we toss the coin, even though the characteristics of environment, we can, in principle, predict what would be the outcome."
